course: dictionary
lesson: 12133
title: Commerce and trade
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Commerce means the buying and selling of goods and services. Look at the words around it to find its meaning. «Ships sailed up the river to bring cloth and spices for commerce.» The ships carried goods to sell, so commerce must mean trade.
explain: Commerce can also mean the whole world of shops, banks and markets in one place. «The square was the centre of commerce in the town.» A busy square full of shops and stalls shows that commerce is about business and trade, not about the place itself.
explain: Commerce is an uncountable noun, so it has no plural. You say «commerce», never «commerces». Phrases like «chamber of commerce» and «e-commerce» use the same idea. A chamber of commerce is a club of local businesses, and e-commerce means buying and selling online.
fact: Commerce means the buying and selling of goods and services.
fact: Commerce can mean the whole world of trade and business in a place, such as shops, banks and markets.
fact: «Commerce» is an uncountable noun, so it has no plural.
fact: «Chamber of commerce» means a club of local businesses.
fact: «E-commerce» means buying and selling goods online.
exercise: «Ships sailed up the river to bring goods for commerce.» What does commerce mean here? | Building new houses | Sailing and swimming | *Buying and selling goods | Singing songs at sea
exercise: «The old market was the centre of commerce in the town.» What is the best meaning of commerce in this sentence? | *Shops, banks and markets where people trade | A quiet place for reading | A tall tower on a hill | A sad song about the sea
exercise: «Online shopping is part of e-commerce.» What is e-commerce? | *Buying and selling things on the internet | Writing letters by email | Fixing broken computers | Reading books on paper
test: «The chamber of commerce meets every month to talk about local shops.» What is a chamber of commerce? | A kind of small boat | A room with a bed | *A club of local businesses | A school for music
test: Which sentence uses commerce correctly? | Commerce were busy every day. | The commerce are busy every day. | Commerces keep the town busy and growing. | *Commerce keeps the town busy and growing.
exam: «Trade and commerce made the port rich.» What does commerce mean in this sentence? | Digging holes in the ground | Painting pictures of boats | *Buying and selling | Sleeping late in the morning
